window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2024-06-3", "source": "2024年06月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence “There is a growing awareness of the importance of digital literacy and skills in today’s world.” You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words. (You should copy the sentence given in quotes at the beginning of your essay. )", "sample": "There is a growing awareness of the importance of digital literacy and skills in today’s world. As study, work\nand daily life move online, the ability to use digital tools wisely has become a basic survival skill rather than an\noptional advantage.\nThis importance is easy to feel in real situations. During the pandemic, all my courses were shifted to online\nplatforms, and only classmates who could navigate video meetings, submit assignments and search for reliable\nsources kept up with the pace. Those who struggled with the technology quickly fell behind, not because they\nwere less clever, but because they lacked the digital skills the new setting demanded. The same gap appears in\nthe job market, where employers now expect graduates to handle data, communicate online and tell trustworthy\ninformation from rumours.\nTherefore, digital literacy is no longer a bonus but a necessity. By actively learning to process information,\nprotect our online safety and keep updating our skills, we can turn technology into a real advantage instead of\na barrier."}, "translation": {"prompt": "扇子自古以来就深受中国人喜爱，但现在已不只是消暑纳凉的工具，而更多地作为艺术品供人欣赏。许多扇子造型优美、做工精良，并绘有山水、花鸟、人物等精美图案，具有很高的艺术价值。中国许多著名画家和书法家喜欢在扇子上作诗绘画，展示其艺术品味。扇子常作为礼物赠予他人，表达美好的祝福和真挚的情感。如今，扇子的实用功能已大为减弱， 但作为一种文化符号和艺术形式，扇子仍然在中国传统文化中扮演着重要角色。", "sample": "Fans have long been cherished by the Chinese people. Today, however, they are no longer merely tools for keeping cool, but are more often appreciated as works of art. Many fans, with graceful shapes and exquisite workmanship, are decorated with delicate patterns of landscapes, flowers and birds, and figures, and thus carry high artistic value. In addition, many famous Chinese painters and calligraphers like to write poems and paint pictures on fans to display their artistic taste. Fans are also often given to others as gifts to convey good wishes and sincere feelings. Today, although the practical function of fans has greatly weakened, the fan, as a cultural symbol and an art form, still plays an important role in traditional Chinese culture."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "A rainbow is a multi-colored, arc-shaped phenomenon that can appear in the sky. The colors of a rainbow are produced by the reflection and  __26__  of light through water droplets(小滴) present in the atmosphere. An observer may  __27__  a rainbow to be located either near or far away, but this phenomenon is not actually located at any specific spot. Instead, the appearance of a rainbow depends entirely upon the position of the observer in  __28__  to the direction of light. In essence, a rainbow is an  __29__  illusion.\nRainbows present a  __30__  made up of seven colors in a specific order. In fact, school children in many English-speaking countries are taught to remember the name \"Roy G. Biv\" as an aid for remembering the colors of a rainbow and their order. \"Roy G. Biv\"  __31__  for: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, and violet. The outer edge of the rainbow arc is red, while the inner edge is violet.\nA rainbow is formed when light (generally sunlight) passes through water droplets  __32__  in the atmosphere. The light waves change direction as they pass through the water droplets, resulting in two processes: reflection and refraction(折射). When light reflects off a water droplet, it simply  __33__  back in the opposite direction from where it  __34__  . When light refracts, it takes a different direction. Some individuals refer to refracted light as \"bent light waves.\" A rainbow is formed because white light enters the water droplet, where it bends in several different directions. When these bent light waves reach the other side of the water droplet, they reflect back out of the droplet instead of  __35__  passing through the water. Since the white light is separated inside of the water, the refracted light appears as separate colors to the human eye.", "matchIntro": "Blame your worthless workdays on meeting recovery syndrome", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Phyllis Hartman knows what it's like to make one's way through the depths of office meeting hell. Managers at one of her former human resources jobs arranged so many meetings that attendees would fall asleep at the table or intentionally arrive late. With hours of her day blocked up with unnecessary meetings, she was often forced to make up her work during overtime. \"I was actually working more hours than I probably would have needed to get the work done,\" says Hartman, who is founder and president of PGHR Consulting in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania."}, {"label": "B", "text": "She isn't alone in her frustration. Between 11 million and 55 million meetings are held each day in the United States, costing most organisations between 7% and 15% of their personnel budgets. Every week, employees spend about six hours in meetings, while the average manager meets for a staggering 23 hours."}, {"label": "C", "text": "And though experts agree that traditional meetings are essential for making certain decisions and developing strategy, some employees view them as one of the most unnecessary parts of the workday. The result is not only hundreds of billions of wasted dollars, but an annoyance of what organisational psychologists call \"meeting recovery syndrome (MRS)\": time spent cooling off and regaining focus after a useless meeting. If you run to the office kitchen to get some relief with colleagues after a frustrating meeting, you're likely experiencing meeting recovery syndrome."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Meeting recovery syndrome is a concept that should be familiar to almost anyone who has held a formal job. It isn't ground-breaking to say workers feel fatigued after a meeting, but only in recent decades have scientists deemed the condition worthy of further investigation. With its links to organisational efficiency and employee wellbeing, MRS has attracted the attention of psychologists aware of the need to understand its precise causes and cures."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Today, in so far as researchers can hypothesise, MRS is most easily understood as a slow renewal of finite mental and physical resources. When an employee sits through an ineffective meeting their brain power is essentially being drained away. Meetings drain vitality if they last too long, fail to engage employees or turn into one-sided lectures. The conservation of resources theory, originally proposed in 1989 by Dr Stevan Hobfoll, states that psychological stress occurs when a person's resources are threatened or lost. When resources are low, a person will shift into defence to conserve their remaining supply. In the case of office meetings, where some of employees' most valuable resources are their focus, alertness and motivation, this can mean an abrupt halt in productivity as they take time to recover."}, {"label": "F", "text": "As humans, when we transition from one task to another on the job—say from sitting in a meeting to doing normal work—it takes an effortful cognitive switch. We must detach ourselves from the previous task and expend significant mental energy to move on. If we are already drained to dangerous levels, then making the mental switch to the next thing is extra tough. It's common to see people cyber-loafing after a frustrating meeting, going and getting coffee, interrupting a colleague and telling them about the meeting, and so on."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Each person's ability to recover from horrible meetings is different. Some can bounce back quickly, while others carry their fatigue until the end of the workday. Yet while no formal MRS studies are currently underway, one can loosely speculate on the length of an average employee's lag time. Switching tasks in a non-MRS condition takes about 10 to 15 minutes. With MRS, it may take as long as 45 minutes on average. It's even worse when a worker has several meetings that are separated by 30 minutes. \"Not enough time to transition in a non-MRS situation to get anything done, and in an MRS situation, not quite enough time to recover for the next meeting,\" says researcher Joseph Allen. \"Then, add the compounding of back-to-back bad meetings and we may have an epidemic on our hands.\""}, {"label": "H", "text": "In an effort to combat the side effects of MRS, Allen, along with researcher Joseph Mroz and colleagues at the University of Nebraska-Omaha, published a study detailing the best ways to avoid common traps, including a concise checklist of do's and don'ts applicable to any workplace. Drawing from around 200 papers to compile their comprehensive list, Mroz and his team may now hold a remedy to the largely undefined problem of MRS."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Mroz says a good place to start is asking ourselves if our meetings are even necessary in the first place. If all that's on the agenda is a quick catch-up, or some non-urgent information sharing, it may better suit the group to send around an email instead. \"The second thing I would always recommend is keep the meeting as small as possible,\" says Mroz. \"If they don't actually have some kind of immediate input, then they can follow up later. They don't need to be sitting in this hour-long meeting.\" Less time in meetings would ultimately lead to more employee engagement in the meetings they do attend, which experts agree is a proven remedy for MRS."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Employees also feel taxed when they are invited together to meetings that don't inspire participation, says Cliff Scott, professor of organisational science. It takes precious time for them to vent their emotions, complain and try to regain focus after a pointless meeting—one of the main traps of MRS. Over time as employees find themselves tied up in more and more unnecessary meetings—and thus dealing with increasing lag times from MRS—the waste of workday hours can feel insulting."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Despite the relative scarcity of research behind the subject, Hartman has taught herself many of the same tricks suggested in Mroz's study, and has come a long way since her days of being stuck with unnecessary meetings. The people she invites to meetings today include not just the essential employees, but also representatives from every department that might have a stake in the issue at hand. Managers like her, who seek input even from non-experts to shape their decisions, can find greater support and cooperation from their workforce, she says."}, {"label": "L", "text": "If an organisation were to apply all 22 suggestions from Mroz and Allen's findings, the most noticeable difference would be a stark decrease in the total number of meetings on the schedule, Mroz says. Less time in meetings would ultimately lead to increased productivity, which is the ultimate objective of convening a meeting. While none of the counter-MRS ideas have been tested empirically yet, Allen says one trick with promise is for employees to identify things that quickly change their mood from negative to positive. As simple as it sounds, finding a personal happy place, going there and then coming straight back to work might be key to facilitating recovery."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Leaders should also see themselves as \"stewards of everyone else's valuable time\", adds Steven Rogelberg, author of The Surprising Science of Meetings. Having the skills to foresee potential traps and treat employees' endurance with care allows leaders to provide effective short-term deterrents to MRS."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Most important, however, is for organisations to awaken to the concept of meetings being flexible, says Allen. By reshaping the way they prioritise employees' time, companies can eliminate the very sources of MRS in their tracks."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Sarcasm and jazz have something surprisingly in common: You know them when you hear them. Sarcasm is mostly understood through tone of voice, which is used to portray the opposite of the literal words. For example, when someone says, \"Well, that's exactly what I need right now,\" their tone can tell you it's not what they need at all.\nMost frequently, sarcasm highlights an irritation or is, quite simply, mean.\nIf you want to be happier and improve your relationships, cut out sarcasm. Why? Because sarcasm is actually hostility disguised as humor.\nDespite smiling outwardly, many people who receive sarcastic comments feel put down and often think the sarcastic person is rude, or contemptible. Indeed, it's not surprising that the origin of the word sarcasm derives from the Greek word \"sarkazein\" which literally means \"to tear or strip the flesh off.\" Hence, it's no wonder that sarcasm is often preceded by the word \"cutting\" and that it hurts.\nWhat's more, since actions strongly determine thoughts and feelings, when a person consistently acts sarcastically it may only serve to heighten their underlying hostility and insecurity. After all, when you come right down to it, sarcasm can be used as a subtle form of bullying—and most bullies are angry, insecure, or cowardly.\nAlternatively, when a person stops voicing negative comments, especially sarcastic ones, they may soon start to feel happier and more self-confident. Also, other people in their life benefit even more because they no longer have to hear the emotionally hurtful language of sarcasm.\nNow, I'm not saying all sarcasm is bad. It may just be better used sparingly—like a potent spice in cooking. Too much of the spice, and the dish will be overwhelmed by it. Similarly, an occasional dash of sarcastic wit can spice up a chat and add an element of humor to it. But a big or steady serving of sarcasm will overwhelm the emotional flavor of any conversation and can taste very bitter to its recipient.\nSo, tone down the sarcasm and work on clever wit instead, which is usually without any hostility and thus more appreciated by those you're communicating with. In essence, sarcasm is easy while true, harmless wit takes talent.\nThus, the main difference between wit and sarcasm is that, as already stated, sarcasm is often hostility disguised as humor. It can be intended to hurt and is often bitter and biting. Witty statements are usually in response to someone's unhelpful remarks or behaviors, and the intent is to untangle and clarify the issue by emphasizing its absurdities. Sarcastic statements are expressed in a cutting manner; witty remarks are delivered with undisguised and harmless humor.", "47": "Sarcasm and jazz have something surprisingly in common: You know them when you hear them. Sarcasm is mostly understood through tone of voice, which is used to portray the opposite of the literal words. For example, when someone says, \"Well, that's exactly what I need right now,\" their tone can tell you it's not what they need at all.\nMost frequently, sarcasm highlights an irritation or is, quite simply, mean.\nIf you want to be happier and improve your relationships, cut out sarcasm. Why? Because sarcasm is actually hostility disguised as humor.\nDespite smiling outwardly, many people who receive sarcastic comments feel put down and often think the sarcastic person is rude, or contemptible. Indeed, it's not surprising that the origin of the word sarcasm derives from the Greek word \"sarkazein\" which literally means \"to tear or strip the flesh off.\" Hence, it's no wonder that sarcasm is often preceded by the word \"cutting\" and that it hurts.\nWhat's more, since actions strongly determine thoughts and feelings, when a person consistently acts sarcastically it may only serve to heighten their underlying hostility and insecurity. After all, when you come right down to it, sarcasm can be used as a subtle form of bullying—and most bullies are angry, insecure, or cowardly.\nAlternatively, when a person stops voicing negative comments, especially sarcastic ones, they may soon start to feel happier and more self-confident. Also, other people in their life benefit even more because they no longer have to hear the emotionally hurtful language of sarcasm.\nNow, I'm not saying all sarcasm is bad. It may just be better used sparingly—like a potent spice in cooking. Too much of the spice, and the dish will be overwhelmed by it. Similarly, an occasional dash of sarcastic wit can spice up a chat and add an element of humor to it. But a big or steady serving of sarcasm will overwhelm the emotional flavor of any conversation and can taste very bitter to its recipient.\nSo, tone down the sarcasm and work on clever wit instead, which is usually without any hostility and thus more appreciated by those you're communicating with. In essence, sarcasm is easy while true, harmless wit takes talent.\nThus, the main difference between wit and sarcasm is that, as already stated, sarcasm is often hostility disguised as humor. It can be intended to hurt and is often bitter and biting. Witty statements are usually in response to someone's unhelpful remarks or behaviors, and the intent is to untangle and clarify the issue by emphasizing its absurdities. Sarcastic statements are expressed in a cutting manner; witty remarks are delivered with undisguised and harmless humor.", "48": "Sarcasm and jazz have something surprisingly in common: You know them when you hear them. Sarcasm is mostly understood through tone of voice, which is used to portray the opposite of the literal words. For example, when someone says, \"Well, that's exactly what I need right now,\" their tone can tell you it's not what they need at all.\nMost frequently, sarcasm highlights an irritation or is, quite simply, mean.\nIf you want to be happier and improve your relationships, cut out sarcasm. Why? Because sarcasm is actually hostility disguised as humor.\nDespite smiling outwardly, many people who receive sarcastic comments feel put down and often think the sarcastic person is rude, or contemptible. Indeed, it's not surprising that the origin of the word sarcasm derives from the Greek word \"sarkazein\" which literally means \"to tear or strip the flesh off.\" Hence, it's no wonder that sarcasm is often preceded by the word \"cutting\" and that it hurts.\nWhat's more, since actions strongly determine thoughts and feelings, when a person consistently acts sarcastically it may only serve to heighten their underlying hostility and insecurity. After all, when you come right down to it, sarcasm can be used as a subtle form of bullying—and most bullies are angry, insecure, or cowardly.\nAlternatively, when a person stops voicing negative comments, especially sarcastic ones, they may soon start to feel happier and more self-confident. Also, other people in their life benefit even more because they no longer have to hear the emotionally hurtful language of sarcasm.\nNow, I'm not saying all sarcasm is bad. It may just be better used sparingly—like a potent spice in cooking. Too much of the spice, and the dish will be overwhelmed by it. Similarly, an occasional dash of sarcastic wit can spice up a chat and add an element of humor to it. But a big or steady serving of sarcasm will overwhelm the emotional flavor of any conversation and can taste very bitter to its recipient.\nSo, tone down the sarcasm and work on clever wit instead, which is usually without any hostility and thus more appreciated by those you're communicating with. In essence, sarcasm is easy while true, harmless wit takes talent.\nThus, the main difference between wit and sarcasm is that, as already stated, sarcasm is often hostility disguised as humor. It can be intended to hurt and is often bitter and biting. Witty statements are usually in response to someone's unhelpful remarks or behaviors, and the intent is to untangle and clarify the issue by emphasizing its absurdities. Sarcastic statements are expressed in a cutting manner; witty remarks are delivered with undisguised and harmless humor.", "49": "Sarcasm and jazz have something surprisingly in common: You know them when you hear them. Sarcasm is mostly understood through tone of voice, which is used to portray the opposite of the literal words. For example, when someone says, \"Well, that's exactly what I need right now,\" their tone can tell you it's not what they need at all.\nMost frequently, sarcasm highlights an irritation or is, quite simply, mean.\nIf you want to be happier and improve your relationships, cut out sarcasm. Why? Because sarcasm is actually hostility disguised as humor.\nDespite smiling outwardly, many people who receive sarcastic comments feel put down and often think the sarcastic person is rude, or contemptible. Indeed, it's not surprising that the origin of the word sarcasm derives from the Greek word \"sarkazein\" which literally means \"to tear or strip the flesh off.\" Hence, it's no wonder that sarcasm is often preceded by the word \"cutting\" and that it hurts.\nWhat's more, since actions strongly determine thoughts and feelings, when a person consistently acts sarcastically it may only serve to heighten their underlying hostility and insecurity. After all, when you come right down to it, sarcasm can be used as a subtle form of bullying—and most bullies are angry, insecure, or cowardly.\nAlternatively, when a person stops voicing negative comments, especially sarcastic ones, they may soon start to feel happier and more self-confident. Also, other people in their life benefit even more because they no longer have to hear the emotionally hurtful language of sarcasm.\nNow, I'm not saying all sarcasm is bad. It may just be better used sparingly—like a potent spice in cooking. Too much of the spice, and the dish will be overwhelmed by it. Similarly, an occasional dash of sarcastic wit can spice up a chat and add an element of humor to it. But a big or steady serving of sarcasm will overwhelm the emotional flavor of any conversation and can taste very bitter to its recipient.\nSo, tone down the sarcasm and work on clever wit instead, which is usually without any hostility and thus more appreciated by those you're communicating with. In essence, sarcasm is easy while true, harmless wit takes talent.\nThus, the main difference between wit and sarcasm is that, as already stated, sarcasm is often hostility disguised as humor. It can be intended to hurt and is often bitter and biting. Witty statements are usually in response to someone's unhelpful remarks or behaviors, and the intent is to untangle and clarify the issue by emphasizing its absurdities. Sarcastic statements are expressed in a cutting manner; witty remarks are delivered with undisguised and harmless humor.", "50": "Sarcasm and jazz have something surprisingly in common: You know them when you hear them. Sarcasm is mostly understood through tone of voice, which is used to portray the opposite of the literal words. For example, when someone says, \"Well, that's exactly what I need right now,\" their tone can tell you it's not what they need at all.\nMost frequently, sarcasm highlights an irritation or is, quite simply, mean.\nIf you want to be happier and improve your relationships, cut out sarcasm. Why? Because sarcasm is actually hostility disguised as humor.\nDespite smiling outwardly, many people who receive sarcastic comments feel put down and often think the sarcastic person is rude, or contemptible. Indeed, it's not surprising that the origin of the word sarcasm derives from the Greek word \"sarkazein\" which literally means \"to tear or strip the flesh off.\" Hence, it's no wonder that sarcasm is often preceded by the word \"cutting\" and that it hurts.\nWhat's more, since actions strongly determine thoughts and feelings, when a person consistently acts sarcastically it may only serve to heighten their underlying hostility and insecurity. After all, when you come right down to it, sarcasm can be used as a subtle form of bullying—and most bullies are angry, insecure, or cowardly.\nAlternatively, when a person stops voicing negative comments, especially sarcastic ones, they may soon start to feel happier and more self-confident. Also, other people in their life benefit even more because they no longer have to hear the emotionally hurtful language of sarcasm.\nNow, I'm not saying all sarcasm is bad. It may just be better used sparingly—like a potent spice in cooking. Too much of the spice, and the dish will be overwhelmed by it. Similarly, an occasional dash of sarcastic wit can spice up a chat and add an element of humor to it. But a big or steady serving of sarcasm will overwhelm the emotional flavor of any conversation and can taste very bitter to its recipient.\nSo, tone down the sarcasm and work on clever wit instead, which is usually without any hostility and thus more appreciated by those you're communicating with. In essence, sarcasm is easy while true, harmless wit takes talent.\nThus, the main difference between wit and sarcasm is that, as already stated, sarcasm is often hostility disguised as humor. It can be intended to hurt and is often bitter and biting. Witty statements are usually in response to someone's unhelpful remarks or behaviors, and the intent is to untangle and clarify the issue by emphasizing its absurdities. Sarcastic statements are expressed in a cutting manner; witty remarks are delivered with undisguised and harmless humor.", "51": "Variability is crucially important for learning new skills. Consider learning how to serve in tennis. Should you always practise serving from the exactly same location on the court, aiming at the same spot? Although practising in more variable conditions will be slower at first, it will likely make you a better tennis player in the end. This is because variability leads to better generalisation of what is learned.\nThis principle is found in many domains, including speech perception and learning categories. For instance, infants will struggle to learn the category \"dog\" if they are only exposed to Chihuahuas, instead of many different kinds of dogs.\n\"There are over ten different names for this basic principle,\" says Limor Raviv, the senior investigator of a recent study. \"Learning from less variable input is often fast, but may fail to generalise to new stimuli.\"\nTo identify key patterns and understand the underlying principles of variability effects, Raviv and her colleagues reviewed over 150 studies on variability and generalisation across fields, including computer science, linguistics, categorisation, visual perception and formal education.\nThe researchers discovered that, across studies, the term variability can refer to at least four different kinds of variability, such as set size and scheduling. \"These four kinds of variability have never been directly compared—which means that we currently don't know which is most effective for learning,\" says Raviv.\nThe impact of variability depends on whether it is relevant to the task or not. But according to the 'Mr. Miyagi principle', practising seemingly unrelated skills may actually benefit learning of other skills.\nBut why does variability impact learning and generalisation? One theory is that more variable input can highlight which aspects of a task are relevant and which are not.\nAnother theory is that greater variability leads to broader generalisations. This is because variability will represent the real world better, including atypical(非典型的) examples.\nA third reason has to do with the way memory works: when training is variable, learners are forced to actively reconstruct their memories.\n\"Understanding the impact of variability is important for literally every aspect of our daily life. Beyond affecting the way we learn language, motor skills, and categories, it even has an impact on our social lives,\" explains Raviv. \"For example, face recognition is affected by whether people grew up in a small community or in a larger community. Exposure to fewer faces during childhood is associated with diminished face memory.\"\n\"We hope this work will spark people's curiosity and generate more work on the topic,\" concludes Raviv. \"Our paper raises a lot of open questions. Can we find similar effects of variability beyond the brain, for instance, in the immune system?\"", "52": "Variability is crucially important for learning new skills. Consider learning how to serve in tennis. Should you always practise serving from the exactly same location on the court, aiming at the same spot? Although practising in more variable conditions will be slower at first, it will likely make you a better tennis player in the end. This is because variability leads to better generalisation of what is learned.\nThis principle is found in many domains, including speech perception and learning categories. For instance, infants will struggle to learn the category \"dog\" if they are only exposed to Chihuahuas, instead of many different kinds of dogs.\n\"There are over ten different names for this basic principle,\" says Limor Raviv, the senior investigator of a recent study. \"Learning from less variable input is often fast, but may fail to generalise to new stimuli.\"\nTo identify key patterns and understand the underlying principles of variability effects, Raviv and her colleagues reviewed over 150 studies on variability and generalisation across fields, including computer science, linguistics, categorisation, visual perception and formal education.\nThe researchers discovered that, across studies, the term variability can refer to at least four different kinds of variability, such as set size and scheduling. \"These four kinds of variability have never been directly compared—which means that we currently don't know which is most effective for learning,\" says Raviv.\nThe impact of variability depends on whether it is relevant to the task or not. But according to the 'Mr. Miyagi principle', practising seemingly unrelated skills may actually benefit learning of other skills.\nBut why does variability impact learning and generalisation? One theory is that more variable input can highlight which aspects of a task are relevant and which are not.\nAnother theory is that greater variability leads to broader generalisations. This is because variability will represent the real world better, including atypical(非典型的) examples.\nA third reason has to do with the way memory works: when training is variable, learners are forced to actively reconstruct their memories.\n\"Understanding the impact of variability is important for literally every aspect of our daily life. Beyond affecting the way we learn language, motor skills, and categories, it even has an impact on our social lives,\" explains Raviv. \"For example, face recognition is affected by whether people grew up in a small community or in a larger community. Exposure to fewer faces during childhood is associated with diminished face memory.\"\n\"We hope this work will spark people's curiosity and generate more work on the topic,\" concludes Raviv. \"Our paper raises a lot of open questions. Can we find similar effects of variability beyond the brain, for instance, in the immune system?\"", "53": "Variability is crucially important for learning new skills. Consider learning how to serve in tennis. Should you always practise serving from the exactly same location on the court, aiming at the same spot? Although practising in more variable conditions will be slower at first, it will likely make you a better tennis player in the end. This is because variability leads to better generalisation of what is learned.\nThis principle is found in many domains, including speech perception and learning categories. For instance, infants will struggle to learn the category \"dog\" if they are only exposed to Chihuahuas, instead of many different kinds of dogs.\n\"There are over ten different names for this basic principle,\" says Limor Raviv, the senior investigator of a recent study. \"Learning from less variable input is often fast, but may fail to generalise to new stimuli.\"\nTo identify key patterns and understand the underlying principles of variability effects, Raviv and her colleagues reviewed over 150 studies on variability and generalisation across fields, including computer science, linguistics, categorisation, visual perception and formal education.\nThe researchers discovered that, across studies, the term variability can refer to at least four different kinds of variability, such as set size and scheduling. \"These four kinds of variability have never been directly compared—which means that we currently don't know which is most effective for learning,\" says Raviv.\nThe impact of variability depends on whether it is relevant to the task or not. But according to the 'Mr. Miyagi principle', practising seemingly unrelated skills may actually benefit learning of other skills.\nBut why does variability impact learning and generalisation? One theory is that more variable input can highlight which aspects of a task are relevant and which are not.\nAnother theory is that greater variability leads to broader generalisations. This is because variability will represent the real world better, including atypical(非典型的) examples.\nA third reason has to do with the way memory works: when training is variable, learners are forced to actively reconstruct their memories.\n\"Understanding the impact of variability is important for literally every aspect of our daily life. Beyond affecting the way we learn language, motor skills, and categories, it even has an impact on our social lives,\" explains Raviv. \"For example, face recognition is affected by whether people grew up in a small community or in a larger community. Exposure to fewer faces during childhood is associated with diminished face memory.\"\n\"We hope this work will spark people's curiosity and generate more work on the topic,\" concludes Raviv. \"Our paper raises a lot of open questions. Can we find similar effects of variability beyond the brain, for instance, in the immune system?\"", "54": "Variability is crucially important for learning new skills. Consider learning how to serve in tennis. Should you always practise serving from the exactly same location on the court, aiming at the same spot? Although practising in more variable conditions will be slower at first, it will likely make you a better tennis player in the end. This is because variability leads to better generalisation of what is learned.\nThis principle is found in many domains, including speech perception and learning categories. For instance, infants will struggle to learn the category \"dog\" if they are only exposed to Chihuahuas, instead of many different kinds of dogs.\n\"There are over ten different names for this basic principle,\" says Limor Raviv, the senior investigator of a recent study. \"Learning from less variable input is often fast, but may fail to generalise to new stimuli.\"\nTo identify key patterns and understand the underlying principles of variability effects, Raviv and her colleagues reviewed over 150 studies on variability and generalisation across fields, including computer science, linguistics, categorisation, visual perception and formal education.\nThe researchers discovered that, across studies, the term variability can refer to at least four different kinds of variability, such as set size and scheduling. \"These four kinds of variability have never been directly compared—which means that we currently don't know which is most effective for learning,\" says Raviv.\nThe impact of variability depends on whether it is relevant to the task or not. But according to the 'Mr. Miyagi principle', practising seemingly unrelated skills may actually benefit learning of other skills.\nBut why does variability impact learning and generalisation? One theory is that more variable input can highlight which aspects of a task are relevant and which are not.\nAnother theory is that greater variability leads to broader generalisations. This is because variability will represent the real world better, including atypical(非典型的) examples.\nA third reason has to do with the way memory works: when training is variable, learners are forced to actively reconstruct their memories.\n\"Understanding the impact of variability is important for literally every aspect of our daily life. Beyond affecting the way we learn language, motor skills, and categories, it even has an impact on our social lives,\" explains Raviv. \"For example, face recognition is affected by whether people grew up in a small community or in a larger community. Exposure to fewer faces during childhood is associated with diminished face memory.\"\n\"We hope this work will spark people's curiosity and generate more work on the topic,\" concludes Raviv. \"Our paper raises a lot of open questions. Can we find similar effects of variability beyond the brain, for instance, in the immune system?\"", "55": "Variability is crucially important for learning new skills. Consider learning how to serve in tennis. Should you always practise serving from the exactly same location on the court, aiming at the same spot? Although practising in more variable conditions will be slower at first, it will likely make you a better tennis player in the end. This is because variability leads to better generalisation of what is learned.\nThis principle is found in many domains, including speech perception and learning categories. For instance, infants will struggle to learn the category \"dog\" if they are only exposed to Chihuahuas, instead of many different kinds of dogs.\n\"There are over ten different names for this basic principle,\" says Limor Raviv, the senior investigator of a recent study. \"Learning from less variable input is often fast, but may fail to generalise to new stimuli.\"\nTo identify key patterns and understand the underlying principles of variability effects, Raviv and her colleagues reviewed over 150 studies on variability and generalisation across fields, including computer science, linguistics, categorisation, visual perception and formal education.\nThe researchers discovered that, across studies, the term variability can refer to at least four different kinds of variability, such as set size and scheduling. \"These four kinds of variability have never been directly compared—which means that we currently don't know which is most effective for learning,\" says Raviv.\nThe impact of variability depends on whether it is relevant to the task or not. But according to the 'Mr. Miyagi principle', practising seemingly unrelated skills may actually benefit learning of other skills.\nBut why does variability impact learning and generalisation? One theory is that more variable input can highlight which aspects of a task are relevant and which are not.\nAnother theory is that greater variability leads to broader generalisations. This is because variability will represent the real world better, including atypical(非典型的) examples.\nA third reason has to do with the way memory works: when training is variable, learners are forced to actively reconstruct their memories.\n\"Understanding the impact of variability is important for literally every aspect of our daily life. Beyond affecting the way we learn language, motor skills, and categories, it even has an impact on our social lives,\" explains Raviv. \"For example, face recognition is affected by whether people grew up in a small community or in a larger community. Exposure to fewer faces during childhood is associated with diminished face memory.\"\n\"We hope this work will spark people's curiosity and generate more work on the topic,\" concludes Raviv. \"Our paper raises a lot of open questions. Can we find similar effects of variability beyond the brain, for instance, in the immune system?\""}, "questions": [{"id": "2024-06-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bounces", "B) completely", "C) dispersion", "D) eccentric", "E) hanging", "F) optical", "G) originates", "H) perceive", "I) permeates", "J) ponder", "K) preceding", "L) recklessly", "M) relation", "N) spectrum", "O) stands"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) dispersion\n名词\n【词性槽】and 并列连接：reflection and ___，并列成分要求同性\n【依据】reflection and dispersion 是描述彩虹成因的标准搭配\nThe colors of a rainbow are produced by the reflection and dispersion of light\nthrough water droplets 光经过水滴时发生「反射 + 色散」是彩虹七色形成的物理原理；\n与下一段 reflection and refraction（折射）共同呼应「光在水滴中的多重作用」。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 N) —— spectrum 也是名词，且与彩虹七色话题强相关，极易抢答\n26。produced by the reflection and __(26)__ of light through water droplets 26 题若\n考生没等到 30 题就抢着填，看到「彩虹 + 光」很容易顺手填 N spectrum；但 26 题靠\nand 并列须填与 reflection 同类的「光学过程」名词（dispersion 色散），spectrum「光\n谱」属结果性名词，且 spectrum 真正归属 30 题 a ___ made up of seven colors。防坑：\n严格执行「用过即划 + 先做高把握空」，让 30 题先把 spectrum 划走。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bounces", "B) completely", "C) dispersion", "D) eccentric", "E) hanging", "F) optical", "G) originates", "H) perceive", "I) permeates", "J) ponder", "K) preceding", "L) recklessly", "M) relation", "N) spectrum", "O) stands"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) perceive\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 may 后接动词原形\n【依据】perceive sb/sth to be ... 「察觉/认为……是……」\nAn observer may perceive a rainbow to be located either near or far away perceive\n后可直接接「宾语 + to be + 状态」，意为「察觉/认为彩虹位于近处或远处」，与下句\nbut this phenomenon is not actually located at any specific spot 形成「主观感知 vs\n客观事实」的转折。\n【竞争词】J) ponder ponder 表「沉思」，不接「宾语 + to be」\nponder 通常作不及物或接 sth/on sth/over sth，不构成「ponder sth to be ...」结构；语义上\n「观察者沉思彩虹位于近处或远处」也极不自然。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bounces", "B) completely", "C) dispersion", "D) eccentric", "E) hanging", "F) optical", "G) originates", "H) perceive", "I) permeates", "J) ponder", "K) preceding", "L) recklessly", "M) relation", "N) spectrum", "O) stands"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) relation\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 in 后 + to 之前，构成 in ___ to 框架\n【依据】固定短语 in relation to = 「相对于、关于」\nthe appearance of a rainbow depends entirely upon the position of the observer in\n__(28)__ to the direction of light. 文意是「彩虹出现完全取决于观察者相对于光线方向的\n位置」，正是 in relation to 的典型用法。\n【竞争词】C) dispersion 无 in dispersion to 这一搭配\ndispersion 表「色散」，与「位置 ↔ 光线方向」的相对关系无关，且不构成固定短语 in\ndispersion to。\nN) spectrum spectrum 指「光谱/范围」，搭配错误\nspectrum 是后文 30 题对应「七色光谱」的名词，与「相对位置」语义不沾边，且无 in\nspectrum to 短语。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bounces", "B) completely", "C) dispersion", "D) eccentric", "E) hanging", "F) optical", "G) originates", "H) perceive", "I) permeates", "J) ponder", "K) preceding", "L) recklessly", "M) relation", "N) spectrum", "O) stands"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) optical\n形容词\n【词性槽】不定冠词 an + 空 + 名词 illusion\n【依据】optical illusion = 「视错觉、光学幻象」固定搭配\nIn essence, a rainbow is an optical illusion. 上文已经指出彩虹「并非真实位于某一地\n点，而是取决于观察者的相对位置」，结论自然是「光学幻象」——optical illusion 是英\n语中表「视错觉」的标准搭配，与全段论证完全吻合。\n【竞争词】D) eccentric eccentric 表「古怪的」，色彩与语境不符\neccentric 多形容人/行为「古怪、反常」，描述彩虹这一自然光学现象时既不准确也不带这种语\n义色彩；eccentric illusion 并非英语习用搭配。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 D) —— eccentric 形态匹配 an + 形容词 + illusion，最容易抢\n29。a rainbow is an __(29)__ illusion. eccentric 以元音 /ɪ/ 开头与 an 适配、作前置定语\n语法完全通；但 optical illusion 才是英语固定搭配「视错觉」，本段全段都在论证「彩虹\n是观察者视觉上的幻象」，与「光学」直接对应。防坑：见 illusion 先想 optical illusion\n这一高频固定搭配，再回头比对其它候选。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bounces", "B) completely", "C) dispersion", "D) eccentric", "E) hanging", "F) optical", "G) originates", "H) perceive", "I) permeates", "J) ponder", "K) preceding", "L) recklessly", "M) relation", "N) spectrum", "O) stands"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) spectrum\n名词\n【词性槽】及物动词 present 后 + 不定冠词 a 后\n【依据】spectrum = 「光谱」，正对应「七色按特定顺序」\nRainbows present a spectrum made up of seven colors in a specific order. 「由七种颜\n色按特定顺序组成」正是 spectrum（光谱）的定义；下文 Roy G. Biv 口诀也是为了记忆这\n一光谱顺序。\n【竞争词】C) dispersion dispersion 强调「色散过程」，非结果性的「七色排列」\ndispersion 指光被分解的「过程/动作」，而本句 made up of seven colors in a specific\norder 描述的是一个由七色组成的「结果性序列」——这是 spectrum 的语义，不是 dispersion\n的语义；且 dispersion 已被 26 题预订（见后）。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bounces", "B) completely", "C) dispersion", "D) eccentric", "E) hanging", "F) optical", "G) originates", "H) perceive", "I) permeates", "J) ponder", "K) preceding", "L) recklessly", "M) relation", "N) spectrum", "O) stands"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) stands\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 \"Roy G. Biv\" 后缺谓语 + 后接 for\n【依据】固定搭配 stand for = 「代表、缩写指代」\n\"Roy G. Biv\" __(31)__ for: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, and violet. 紧随其后\n的就是七色英文首字母对应的全称，文意正是「Roy G. Biv 这一串字母代表红橙黄绿蓝靛\n紫」，stand for 是固定搭配，意为「代表 / 是……的缩写」，与语境完全契合。\n【竞争词】G) originates originate 后不接 for\noriginate 表「起源于」，常搭 originate from/in，无 originate for 这一搭配；语义也讲不通\n——「Roy G. Biv 起源于红橙黄……」不成立。\nH) perceive 形态不符 + 搭配不通\nperceive 非单三，且 perceive for 不是固定搭配；它的宾语应是被感知的对象（如本文 27 题用\n法），与此处「代表七色」不符。\nI) permeates permeate 意为「渗透」\npermeate 表液体/气味/影响渗透弥漫，搭配介词通常是 through/into，与「字母代表颜色」毫\n不相干。\nJ) ponder 形态不符 + 语义不通\nponder 非单三；其义为「沉思」，主语「Roy G. Biv」是个口诀，不会「沉思」，逻辑不\n成立。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bounces", "B) completely", "C) dispersion", "D) eccentric", "E) hanging", "F) optical", "G) originates", "H) perceive", "I) permeates", "J) ponder", "K) preceding", "L) recklessly", "M) relation", "N) spectrum", "O) stands"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) hanging\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词 water droplets 后置修饰 + 空后接 in the atmosphere 介词短语\n【依据】hanging in the atmosphere = 「悬浮在大气中」\nwater droplets hanging in the atmosphere 与第 1 段 water droplets(小滴) present in\nthe atmosphere 同义改写，hanging 形象描绘水滴「悬浮 / 飘在」空中的状态，与上下文\n「光穿过水滴形成彩虹」语境完全一致。\n【竞争词】K) preceding preceding 表「在前的、先前的」，语义与空间状态无关\npreceding 多用于时间或顺序「前一个、之前的」（如 in the preceding paragraph），不能\n用来描述水滴「悬浮在大气中」的物理状态。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bounces", "B) completely", "C) dispersion", "D) eccentric", "E) hanging", "F) optical", "G) originates", "H) perceive", "I) permeates", "J) ponder", "K) preceding", "L) recklessly", "M) relation", "N) spectrum", "O) stands"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) bounces\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 it（= 光）后缺谓语，且与并列分句时态一致\n【依据】bounce back = 「反弹回去」，与 reflect 同义改写\nWhen light reflects off a water droplet, it simply bounces back in the opposite\ndirection from where it __(34)__. 上文 reflects 已给出动作，本句用 simply bounces\nback 同义改写以「反弹回来」的形象语言再说一遍，与 in the opposite direction 表方向\n反向严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】G) originates originate 表「起源」，与「反弹回来」语义相反\noriginate 强调「光从某处发源」，与本句 reflect / back / opposite direction 的「返回」语义\n方向相反，不可选。\nI) permeates permeate 是「渗透、弥漫」，与 bounce back 行为完全相反\npermeate 表向内/向四周扩散，与「沿反方向反弹回去」（in the opposite direction）的物理\n路径冲突。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bounces", "B) completely", "C) dispersion", "D) eccentric", "E) hanging", "F) optical", "G) originates", "H) perceive", "I) permeates", "J) ponder", "K) preceding", "L) recklessly", "M) relation", "N) spectrum", "O) stands"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) originates\n动词\n【词性槽】from where 引导的地点从句缺谓语 + 主语 it 单三\n【依据】from where it originates = 「从它发源的地方」\nit simply bounces back in the opposite direction from where it __(34)__. 光被反射后沿\n反方向回到「它最初出发/发源的方向」——originate 表「起源、发源」与 from 搭配自然\n（originate from），完美对应「反弹回光线最初来的地方」。\n【竞争词】I) permeates permeate 强调「渗透、弥漫」，与「反弹回起点」语义不符\npermeate 描述向各方向扩散浸入，无「起点 / 来处」之意；from where it permeates 既不构\n成习惯表达，也违背物理「光从原方向反弹回去」的描述。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) bounces", "B) completely", "C) dispersion", "D) eccentric", "E) hanging", "F) optical", "G) originates", "H) perceive", "I) permeates", "J) ponder", "K) preceding", "L) recklessly", "M) relation", "N) spectrum", "O) stands"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) completely\n副词\n【词性槽】instead of 后接动名词 passing，空在 of 与 passing 之间作状语\n【依据】completely = 「完全地」，与 instead of 形成「反射 vs 完全穿透」的对照\nthey reflect back out of the droplet instead of __(35)__ passing through the water. 上\n文说光在水滴另一侧「反射回来」，而非「完全穿过水滴」——completely 强调「彻底/全\n部」穿过去，与 reflect back 构成自然对比。\n【竞争词】L) recklessly recklessly 含「鲁莽」感情色彩，与物理过程不符\nrecklessly 形容人的行为「不顾后果」，带主观贬义；光是物理对象，无所谓「鲁莽地穿过\n水」，逻辑荒谬。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Although employees are said to be fatigued by meetings, the condition has not been considered worthy of further research until recently.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】近期才被认为值得深入研究\n【定位】It isn't ground-breaking to say workers feel fatigued after a meeting, but only in recent\ndecades have scientists deemed the condition worthy of further investigation.\n说员工开完会感到疲惫并不是什么新鲜事，但只有在最近几十年里，科学家才认为这一状况\n值得进一步研究。\n【改写】employees are said to be fatigued by meetings ↔\nIt isn't ground-breaking to say workers feel fatigued after a meeting\n员工开会感到疲惫的同义改写\nthe condition has not been considered worthy of further research until recently ↔\nonly in recent decades have scientists deemed the condition worthy of further investigation\n时间副词与同根词等值替换", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Mroz and his team compiled a list of what to do and what not to do to remedy the problem of MRS.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Mroz 团队编「该做与不该做」清单\n【定位】In an effort to combat the side effects of MRS, Allen, along with researcher Joseph Mroz\nand colleagues at the University of Nebraska-Omaha, published a study detailing the best\nways to avoid common traps, including a concise checklist of do's and don'ts applicable to\nany workplace.\n为了对抗 MRS 的副作用，Allen 与研究者 Joseph Mroz 及内布拉斯加大学奥马哈分校的同\n事们发表了一项研究，详述躲开常见陷阱的最佳做法，并附上一份适用于任何工作场所的\n「该做与不该做」简明清单。\n【改写】compiled a list of what to do and what not to do ↔ a concise checklist of do's and don'ts\n习语展开与名词同义替换\nto remedy the problem of MRS ↔\nmay now hold a remedy to the largely undefined problem of MRS\nremedy 加 MRS 同根词原样复现\n【辨邻】 I I 段虽提到 Mroz 与具体建议，但讲的是 asking ourselves if our meetings are even\nnecessary 与 keep the meeting as small as possible 等内容，并未承载题干所问的\n「编了一份 do's and don'ts 清单」这一编纂动作，故不是源头段。\nMroz says a good place to start is asking ourselves if our meetings are even necessary in the\nfirst place", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Companies can get rid of the root cause of MRS if they give priority to workers' time.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】把员工时间放在优先位，根除 MRS\n【定位】By reshaping the way they prioritise employees' time, companies can eliminate the very\nsources of MRS in their tracks.\n通过重塑公司对员工时间的优先排序方式，企业可以在源头就消除 MRS 的根因。\n【改写】get rid of the root cause of MRS ↔ eliminate the very sources of MRS in their tracks\n动词同义加名词同义双替换\ngive priority to workers' time ↔ prioritise employees' time\n动词短语与同义单词\n【辨邻】 L the most noticeable difference would be a stark decrease in the total number\nof meetings on the schedule 表达的是「会议数量下降」这一可观察结果，并未涉及\n「根源」或「优先员工时间」，与 Q38 问的「根除」不是同一指向。\nthe most noticeable difference would be a stark decrease in the total number of meetings on\nthe schedule", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "If workers are exhausted to a dangerous degree, it is extremely hard for them to transition to the next task.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】危险疲惫程度下切换任务极其困难\n【定位】If we are already drained to dangerous levels, then making the mental switch to the next thing\nis extra tough.\n如果我们已经被消耗到危险的程度，那么把心思切换到下一件事就格外吃力。\n【改写】If workers are exhausted to a dangerous degree ↔ If we are already drained to dangerous levels\n条件句的同义词逐对替换\nit is extremely hard for them to transition to the next task ↔\nmaking the mental switch to the next thing is extra tough\n形容词与动名词的同义改写\n【辨邻】 E E 段虽强调 Meetings drain vitality 与 shift into defence，但讨论的是 Hobfoll 资源\n保存理论及生产力骤停的整体机制，并未承载「已经被耗到危险程度时切换格外艰难」\n这一具体论断。\nMeetings drain vitality if they last too long, fail to engage employees or turn into one-sided lectures", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Employees in America spend a lot of time attending meetings while the number of hours managers meet is several times more.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】美国员工开会时长与经理时长数倍对比\n【定位】Every week, employees spend about six hours in meetings, while the average manager meets\nfor a staggering 23 hours.\n每周，员工大约花六小时在会议上，而经理们平均开会的时长达到惊人的 23 小时。\n【改写】Employees in America spend a lot of time attending meetings ↔\nEvery week, employees spend about six hours in meetings\n地理背景加模糊量化的同义对应\nthe number of hours managers meet is several times more ↔\nthe average manager meets for a staggering 23 hours\n倍数关系与具体数字比较", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Phyllis Hartman has learned by herself many of the ways Mroz suggested in his study and made remarkable success in freeing herself from unnecessary meetings.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Phyllis Hartman 自学 Mroz 招数\n【定位】Despite the relative scarcity of research behind the subject, Hartman has taught herself many\nof the same tricks suggested in Mroz's study, and has come a long way since her days of\nbeing stuck with unnecessary meetings.\n尽管这一主题的研究相对匮乏，Hartman 还是自学了 Mroz 研究中所建议的许多招式，并\n且从当年被无谓会议困住的日子算起，已经取得了相当大的进步。\n【改写】Phyllis Hartman has learned by herself many of the ways Mroz suggested in his study ↔\nHartman has taught herself many of the same tricks suggested in Mroz's study\n反身动词与名词同义替换\nmade remarkable success in freeing herself from unnecessary meetings ↔\nhas come a long way since her days of being stuck with unnecessary meetings\n习语与同义解释\n【辨邻】 A A 段虽然出现 Phyllis Hartman，但讲的是她当年在前一份人力资源工作中被会议困\n扰、需要 make up her work during overtime 的早期经历，并未涉及「自学 Mroz\n研究中的招数」这一进步过程，故不能作答。\nI was actually working more hours than I probably would have needed to get the work done", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "When meetings continue too long or don't engage employees, they deplete vitality.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】会议太长或不能让员工投入会耗尽 vitality\n【定位】Meetings drain vitality if they last too long, fail to engage employees or turn into one-\nsided lectures.\n如果会议太久、无法吸引员工参与，或变成单方面讲座，就会耗尽员工的活力。\n【改写】When meetings continue too long or don't engage employees ↔\nif they last too long, fail to engage employees\n条件从句各分支的同义对应\nthey deplete vitality ↔ Meetings drain vitality\n罕见动词宾语搭配的同义替换\n【辨邻】 F F 段确实在讨论被 drained 后的状态，但承载的是 making the mental switch to the\nnext thing is extra tough 这一切换难度，并未列出「会议太长或不能吸引员工」这一\n耗尽的条件，故不是 Q42 的源头段。\nIf we are already drained to dangerous levels, then making the mental switch to the next thing is\nextra tough", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "When the time of meetings is reduced, employees will be more engaged in the meetings they do participate in.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】会议时间减少，员工在所开会议中更投入\n【定位】Less time in meetings would ultimately lead to more employee engagement in the meetings\nthey do attend, which experts agree is a proven remedy for MRS.\n在会议上花的时间更少，最终会让员工在他们真正参加的那些会议中更投入，专家们认为这\n是 MRS 已被证实的解药。\n【改写】When the time of meetings is reduced ↔ Less time in meetings\n被动与名词短语的等值\nemployees will be more engaged in the meetings they do participate in ↔\nmore employee engagement in the meetings they do attend\n名词化与动词被动加同义动词\n【辨邻】 L Less time in meetings would ultimately lead to increased productivity 在 L 段\n也出现，但 L 段的中心论断是 a stark decrease in the total number of meetings\non the schedule，强调「数量」变化，并未明确承载「在所参加会议中更投入」这一\n表述。\nLess time in meetings would ultimately lead to increased productivity, which is the ultimate\nobjective of convening a meeting", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Some employees consider meetings one of the most dispensable parts of the workday.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】员工视会议为工作日最可有可无的环节之一\n【定位】And though experts agree that traditional meetings are essential for making certain decisions\nand developing strategy, some employees view them as one of the most unnecessary parts of\nthe workday.\n尽管专家们一致认为传统会议对某些决策和战略制定是必不可少的，但仍有一些员工把会议\n视为工作日中最不必要的环节之一。\n【改写】Some employees consider meetings one of the most dispensable parts of the workday ↔\nsome employees view them as one of the most unnecessary parts of the workday\n形容词同义加整句平移", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "According to Mroz, if all his suggestions were applied, a very obvious change would be a steep decrease in the number of meetings scheduled.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】若 Mroz 全部建议落地，会议数会陡降\n【定位】If an organisation were to apply all 22 suggestions from Mroz and Allen's findings, the most\nnoticeable difference would be a stark decrease in the total number of meetings on the\nschedule, Mroz says.\nMroz 说，假如一个组织把 Mroz 与 Allen 研究中的所有 22 条建议都落实，最显著的变化\n会是日程表上会议总数的大幅下降。\n【改写】if all his suggestions were applied ↔\nIf an organisation were to apply all 22 suggestions from Mroz and Allen's findings\n虚拟假设的主被动改写\na very obvious change would be a steep decrease in the number of meetings scheduled ↔\nthe most noticeable difference would be a stark decrease in the total number of meetings on\nthe schedule\n形容词同义加介词短语与分词\n【辨邻】 N companies can eliminate the very sources of MRS in their tracks 谈的是根除\nMRS，并未提及「会议日程表上数量的陡降」，与 Q45 的中心结果不匹配，故不是源\n头段。\nBy reshaping the way they prioritise employees' time, companies can eliminate the very sources of\nMRS in their tracks", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "Why does the author say sarcasm and jazz have something surprisingly in common?", "options": ["A) Both are recognized when heard.", "B) Both have exactly the same tone.", "C) Both mean the opposite of what they appear to.", "D) Both have hidden in them an evident irritation."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Q46 用 Why does the author say... 询问作者把 sarcasm 与 jazz 类比的具体依据，不是问\n主旨也不是问推断。\n【拆句】Q46 主干为 Why does the author say + 宾语从句 sarcasm and jazz have something\nsurprisingly in common；宾语从句里 something 是 have 的宾语，surprisingly in\ncommon 整体作 something 的后置修饰；结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Sarcasm and jazz have something surprisingly in common: You know them when you\nhear them.\n讽刺和爵士乐有一个出人意料的共同点：你一听就能认出它们。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nYou know them when you hear them. ↔ Both are recognized when heard.\nBoth are recognized when heard. 是 You know them when you hear them. 的标准\n被动同义改写，与预判完全一致。\nB) 无中生有\n原文只说\"sarcasm 借 tone of voice 传达\"，从未说 sarcasm 与 jazz 拥有相同语气。\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文 Sarcasm is mostly understood through tone of voice, which is used to\nportray the opposite of the literal words. 把\"言反其意\"只归于 sarcasm，与 jazz\n无关。\nD) 张冠李戴\nMost frequently, sarcasm highlights an irritation 只说 sarcasm 反映 irritation，与\njazz 无关。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "How do many people feel when they hear sarcastic comments?", "options": ["A) They feel hostile towards the sarcastic person.", "B) They feel belittled and disrespected.", "C) They feel a strong urge to retaliate.", "D) They feel incapable of disguising their irritation."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Q47 用 How do many people feel 直接问感受类细节，答案应是原文情绪句的同义改写。\n【拆句】Q47 How 引导特殊疑问，主干 do many people feel，when 引导状语从句 they hear\nsarcastic comments；结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Despite smiling outwardly, many people who receive sarcastic comments feel put down and\noften think the sarcastic person is rude, or contemptible.\n尽管表面带笑，许多人在听到讽刺的话时仍会感觉被贬低，并常常认为讽刺他们的人粗鲁或\n可鄙。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\n原文 sarcasm is actually hostility disguised as humor 把 hostility 归于讽刺者，不是\n接收者的感受。\nB) ✓ 正确\nfeel put down ↔ They feel belittled and disrespected.\nthink the sarcastic person is rude, or contemptible ↔ disrespected\nThey feel belittled and disrespected. 恰是 feel put down 的双词同义改写，与预判\n一致。\nC) 无中生有\n原文从未提及接收者想报复，文章主张反而是\"停掉讽刺\"以缓和关系。\nD) 与原文相反\nDespite smiling outwardly 恰恰是说接收者表面在笑——即\"能掩饰\"，与 incapable\nof disguising 相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What happens when a person consistently acts sarcastically?", "options": ["A) They feel their dignity greatly heightened.", "B) They feel increasingly insecure and hostile.", "C) They endure hostility under the disguise of humor.", "D) They taste bitterness even in pleasant interactions."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Q48 问\"当某人持续讽刺时会发生什么\"，是原文一个具体因果陈述，属细节题。\n【拆句】Q48 What happens 主干 + when a person consistently acts sarcastically 时间状语从\n句；副词 consistently 修饰 acts，无陷阱。\n【定位】when a person consistently acts sarcastically it may only serve to heighten their underlying\nhostility and insecurity.\n当一个人一贯地讽刺人，这种行为只会加深他内心潜藏的敌意与不安全感。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文加深的是 hostility/insecurity 等负面情绪，而非 dignity（尊严）；正相反，长期\n讽刺者被视为隐性霸凌者，尊严下滑。\nB) ✓ 正确\nheighten their underlying hostility and insecurity ↔\nThey feel increasingly insecure and hostile.\nThey feel increasingly insecure and hostile. 把\"程度加深\"用 increasingly 还原，把名\n词敌意/不安全感转回形容词，恰是预判同义。\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文 hostility disguised as humor 的施事是讽刺者本人；这里把\"在 humor 伪装下被\n迫忍受 hostility\"按到讽刺者身上，搞反了角色。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文 can taste very bitter to its recipient 的 bitter 是接收者的感觉，且语境是\"过量\n讽刺\"，从未说\"持续讽刺者在愉快互动中也尝苦\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does the author say about people quitting sarcastic comments?", "options": ["A) It makes others happier and more self-confident.", "B) It restrains them from being irritating and bullying.", "C) It benefits not only themselves but also those around them.", "D) It shields them from negative comments and outright hostility."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】Q49 用 What does the author say about... 询问作者对\"停讽\"的具体陈述，属对应一句原\n文的细节题。\n【拆句】Q49 主干 What does the author say + about 介词短语；about 的宾语是动名词短语\npeople quitting sarcastic comments；结构无陷阱。\n【定位】Alternatively, when a person stops voicing negative comments, especially sarcastic ones, they\nmay soon start to feel happier and more self-confident. Also, other people in their life benefit\neven more because they no longer have to hear the emotionally hurtful language of sarcasm.\n反过来说，当一个人不再说讽刺等负面话语时，他自己很快会更快乐、更自信；而且，他生\n活中的其他人受益更多，因为他们再也不必听到讽刺这种伤人的话。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\n原文 happier and more self-confident 的主语是讽刺者本人，A 只说让 others 更乐更\n自信，丢掉了\"自己\"那一半。\nB) 无中生有\n原文虽在 can be used as a subtle form of bullying 提及 bullying，但 P6 描述停讽效\n果时根本没出现\"约束自己别再霸凌\"的说法。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthey may soon start to feel happier and more self-confident ↔ not only themselves\nother people in their life benefit even more ↔ but also those around them\nIt benefits not only themselves but also those around them. 用 not only...but also\n同时呼应 may soon start to feel happier 与 other people in their life benefit even\nmore，与预判一致。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文是\"停讽者不再向外发讽刺、让他人不必听到\"，并非\"屏蔽讽刺者本人免受外界负面\n话语\"，方向相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is the chief difference between a speaker's wit and sarcasm?", "options": ["A) Their clarity.", "B) Their appreciation.", "C) Their emphasis.", "D) Their intention."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】Q50 直接问\"主要区别是什么\"，原文有总结句给出，属细节题而非主旨题。\n【拆句】Q50 主干 What is the chief difference + 后置介词短语 between a speaker's wit and\nsarcasm；between 引导比较对象；结构无陷阱。\n【定位】Thus, the main difference between wit and sarcasm is that, as already stated, sarcasm is often\nhostility disguised as humor. It can be intended to hurt and is often bitter and biting. Witty\nstatements are usually in response to someone's unhelpful remarks or behaviors, and the\nintent is to untangle and clarify the issue by emphasizing its absurdities.\n于是，机智与讽刺的主要区别在于：讽刺常是\"敌意伪装成幽默\"，可能意在伤人；而机智之\n语意在厘清问题、点出其荒谬之处。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nuntangle and clarify the issue 只出现在 wit 一侧；讽刺侧从未谈到 clarity 这一\n维度。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 more appreciated by those you're communicating with 谈的是\"听者是否欣\n赏\"，是结果而非区别本身。\nC) 以偏概全\nby emphasizing its absurdities 中 emphasizing 仅是 wit 实现意图的手段，不是对比\n双方共有的维度。\nD) ✓ 正确\nIt can be intended to hurt ↔ Their intention.\nthe intent is to untangle and clarify the issue ↔ Their intention.\nTheir intention. 把原文 sarcasm 的 be intended to hurt 与 wit 的 intent is to\nuntangle and clarify 抽象为共同维度 intention，与预判一致。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the passage say about infants learning the category \"dog\" if they are exposed to Chihuahuas only?", "options": ["A) They will encounter some degree of difficulty.", "B) They will try to categorise other objects first.", "C) They will prefer Chihuahuas to other dog species.", "D) They will imagine Chihuahuas in various conditions."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage say about infants learning the category \"dog\" if they are\nexposed to Chihuahuas only? 以 What does the passage say about... 起手，要求复述\n原文事实，没有 infer/suggest 等推断信号。\n【拆句】Q51 是 What 引导的特殊疑问句，主干 What does the passage say about infants\nlearning the category \"dog\"，其中 learning the category \"dog\" 是 v-ing 短语做\ninfants 的后置定语；末尾 if they are exposed to Chihuahuas only 是条件状语从句，被\n动语态 are exposed to 意为“只接触到”，是理解前提。\n【定位】For instance, infants will struggle to learn the category \"dog\" if they are only exposed to\nChihuahuas, instead of many different kinds of dogs.\n举例来说，如果婴儿只接触吉娃娃，而不是接触多种不同种类的狗，他们将很难学会“狗”这\n一类别。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nstruggle to learn the category \"dog\" ↔ encounter some degree of difficulty\n预判“会有学习困难”与 encounter some degree of difficulty 在语义上严格对应，无需\n推断，直接锁定 A。\nB) 无中生有\nThey will try to categorise other objects first 在 P2 找不到任何对应——原文只说他\n们 struggle to learn“狗”，未说他们会先去分类别的东西。\nC) 偷换概念\nThey will prefer Chihuahuas to other dog species 把原文“只接触到吉娃娃”曲解为“偏\n好吉娃娃”——原文谈的是输入受限导致学习困难，与个人偏好无关。\nD) 张冠李戴\nThey will imagine Chihuahuas in various conditions 把第 1 段“在多变条件下练习”的\n概念硬塞到婴儿身上——原文中婴儿并不会主动 imagine，“in various conditions”是\n上一段网球训练的语境。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What does Raviv say about the four different kinds of variability?", "options": ["A) Which of them is most relevant to the task at hand is to be confirmed.", "B) Why they have an impact on learning is far from being understood.", "C) Why they have never been directly compared remains a mystery.", "D) Which of them is most conducive to learning is yet to be identified."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does Raviv say about the four different kinds of variability? 用 say about 句式\n问 Raviv 对四种 variability 的明确表述，是事实复述而非推断。\n【拆句】Q52 主干 What does Raviv say，介词短语 about the four different kinds of variability\n限定话题范围；句中 the four different kinds of 含定冠词 the，提示原文已先有这“四种”\n的提及。\n【定位】\"These four kinds of variability have never been directly compared—which means that we\ncurrently don't know which is most effective for learning,\" says Raviv.\nRaviv 说：“这四种 variability 从未被直接比较过——这意味着我们目前还不知道哪种对学\n习最有效。”\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nWhich of them is most relevant to the task at hand is to be confirmed 把原文的\nmost effective for learning（对学习最有效）偷换为 most relevant to the task at\nhand（对手头任务最相关）——relevant to the task 是第 6 段的话题，不是第 5 段\nRaviv 引语的内容。\nB) 答非所问\nWhy they have an impact on learning is far from being understood 是关于“机制为\n何”的论断，不是 Raviv 在第 5 段就四种 variability 所说的话；况且第 7–9 段恰恰给出\n了三种 theory，“远未被理解”与原文也不符。\nC) 过度推断\nWhy they have never been directly compared remains a mystery 把原文 These\nfour kinds of variability have never been directly compared 的事实（“未被比较”）\n扭曲成“为何未被比较仍是谜”——原文从未说“为何未被比较”是个谜。\nD) ✓ 正确\nwe currently don't know which is most effective for learning ↔\nWhich of them is most conducive to learning is yet to be identified\nmost effective for learning ↔ most conducive to learning\n无论是“目前不知→尚待确认”的含蓄否定，还是“最有效→最有助益”的形容词替换，都精\n准落在 D 项措辞上，锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "How does one of the theories explain the importance of variability for learning new skills?", "options": ["A) Learners regard variable training as typical of what happens in the real world.", "B) Learners receiving variable training are compelled to reorganise their memories.", "C) Learners pay attention to the relevant aspects of a task and ignore those irrelevant.", "D) Learners focus on related skills instead of wasting time and effort on unrelated ones."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】How does one of the theories explain the importance of variability for learning new\nskills? 问“某个理论怎么解释”，要求复述原文中三个理论之一的具体内容，仍属细节题。\n【拆句】Q53 How 引导特殊疑问，主干 How does one of the theories explain the\nimportance，of 短语 of variability for learning new skills 限定 importance，并不影响\n理解。注意 one of the theories 用单数动词 explain，提示只问其中一个理论。\n【定位】A third reason has to do with the way memory works: when training is variable, learners are\nforced to actively reconstruct their memories.\n第三个原因与记忆的运作方式有关：当训练具有多变性时，学习者被迫主动地重构他们的\n记忆。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nLearners regard variable training as typical of what happens in the real world 把\nvariability will represent the real world better, including atypical examples 颠倒\n——原文是“多变训练能更好地代表真实世界（包含非典型例子）”，并非“学习者把多变\n训练视为典型”。\nB) ✓ 正确\nlearners are forced to actively reconstruct their memories ↔\nLearners receiving variable training are compelled to reorganise their memories\nwhen training is variable ↔ receiving variable training\n条件状语从句→v-ing 定语、被动词组→被动词组、动词短语→动词短语三层替换齐全，\n正确项 B 与定位句构成一对一映射。\nC) 偷换概念\nLearners pay attention to the relevant aspects of a task and ignore those\nirrelevant 把 more variable input can highlight which aspects of a task are\nrelevant and which are not 偷换主语——原文是“多变输入会突显相关/不相关方面”，\n并非“学习者主动关注/忽略”。\nD) 与原文相反\nLearners focus on related skills instead of wasting time and effort on unrelated\nones 把 practising seemingly unrelated skills may actually benefit learning of\nother skills 的意思颠倒——原文是“练习看似无关的技能也能助益”，选项却说“专注相关\n技能而不浪费在无关技能上”，恰恰相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the passage say about face recognition?", "options": ["A) People growing up in a small community may find it easy to remember familiar faces.", "B) Face recognition has a significant impact on literally every aspect of our social lives.", "C) People growing up in a large community can readily recognise any individual faces.", "D) The size of the community people grow up in impacts their face recognition ability."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage say about face recognition? 仍是 What does the passage say\nabout 句式，问原文如何陈述 face recognition 这一事实。\n【拆句】Q54 结构与第 51 题同款，主干 What does the passage say about face recognition? 无\n从句无否定，平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】For example, face recognition is affected by whether people grew up in a small community or\nin a larger community.\n例如，面孔识别会受到人们是在小社区还是大社区长大这一因素的影响。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nPeople growing up in a small community may find it easy to remember familiar\nfaces 给“小社区”贴上“易记熟人脸”的标签，但 Exposure to fewer faces during\nchildhood is associated with diminished face memory 恰恰说“接触少→记忆减弱”，\n与 A 的方向相反；且原文没区分 familiar faces。\nB) 张冠李戴\nFace recognition has a significant impact on literally every aspect of our social\nlives 把“variability 影响每一方面”安到 face recognition 头上——原文\nUnderstanding the impact of variability is important for literally every aspect of\nour daily life 的主语是 variability，不是 face recognition。\nC) 过度推断\nPeople growing up in a large community can readily recognise any individual\nfaces 用 any individual faces 把原文中性的“识别能力受影响”夸大为“能轻松识别任何\n个人面孔”，是绝对化推断。\nD) ✓ 正确\nface recognition is affected by whether people grew up in a small community or in a larger\ncommunity\n↔ The size of the community people grow up in impacts their face recognition ability\nD 既保留了“社区规模→识别能力”的因果，也保留了“人群”层面的中性陈述，没有像 A/C\n那样把因果方向具体化、特例化，最贴近原文。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2024-06-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does Raviv hope to do with their research work?", "options": ["A) Highlight which aspects of a task are relevant and which are not to learning a skill.", "B) Use the principle of variability in teaching seemingly unrelated skills in education.", "C) Arouse people's interest in variability and stimulate more research on the topic.", "D) Apply the principle of variability to such fields of study as the immune system."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does Raviv hope to do with their research work? 问 Raviv 对其研究的具体期望，\n要求复述原文具体表述，不需推断。\n【拆句】Q55 主干 What does Raviv hope to do，with 短语 with their research work 是状语，\n说明 do 的对象是 research work。their 在此泛指研究团队，非语法陷阱。\n【定位】\"We hope this work will spark people's curiosity and generate more work on the topic,\"\nconcludes Raviv.\nRaviv 总结说：“我们希望这项工作能激发人们的好奇心，并产生更多关于这一主题的\n研究。”\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nHighlight which aspects of a task are relevant and which are not to learning a\nskill 是 more variable input can highlight which aspects of a task are relevant and\nwhich are not 中第 1 个理论的措辞，并非 Raviv 在末段表达的研究愿景。\nB) 答非所问\nUse the principle of variability in teaching seemingly unrelated skills in education\n取自 practising seemingly unrelated skills may actually benefit learning of other\nskills 的 Mr. Miyagi 原则，也并非 Raviv 末段表达的愿景。\nC) ✓ 正确\nspark people's curiosity ↔ Arouse people's interest in variability\ngenerate more work on the topic ↔ stimulate more research on the topic\nC 把原文两个并列愿望 spark...curiosity 与 generate...work 都同义改写并对齐，没有\n遗漏其中任何一个，符合“并列动作全保留”原则。\nD) 过度推断\nApply the principle of variability to such fields of study as the immune system 把\nCan we find similar effects of variability beyond the brain, for instance, in the\nimmune system? 这一开放性问题当成 Raviv 的具体计划——原文是疑问句、是抛出问\n题，并非“打算应用到免疫系统”。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2024-06-2"});
